# Skill Name

One paragraph on what this skill is for, written for a person. Same scope as
the description, but as prose rather than a trigger. Address whoever is
reading ("you"); never a named person or a department. Roles only where a
role changes what to do.

## When to use this skill

Concrete situations and phrasings that should open this skill. Keep this in
step with the description; the description is what the assistant reads to
decide, this section is what a person reads to understand.

## When NOT to use this skill

Every exclusion names where the thing lives instead.

- Adjacent task A: use [sibling-skill] instead
- Adjacent task B: handle directly, no skill needed

## What to know

The judgment. Numbered steps, short tables, before-and-after pairs, whatever
teaches best. Keep it to what a senior colleague would tell a new hire;
leave the manual in its source and point at it.

If the skill is short, everything goes here. If it grows, keep this section
as the overview and move detail into files the body names:

- See `references/topic.md` for [topic]
- Run `scripts/do_thing.py` to [do the thing]

## Examples

Concrete input and output, or before and after. Use the agreed placeholder
names, never a real customer or colleague, and never the people who were in
the interview. Examples get copied, so make sure each one obeys every rule
in the plugin, not only this skill's.

## Notes

Anything else: gotchas, related skills, what this skill deliberately leaves
out. Optional.
